.SH "BUGS"
\fBexit \-1\fR is used for error exits\. POSIX does not guarantee the behaviour of negative exit codes; most shells interpret \fB\-1\fR as 255, but this is not portable\.
.P
The \fB/proc/<pid>\fR check is Linux\-specific and will not work on macOS or other non\-Linux systems\.
.P
\fB$RECORD_START_PROGRAM\fR and \fB$RECORD_PID_FILE\fR are unquoted throughout; values containing spaces or shell metacharacters may cause unexpected behaviour\.
.P
If two instances of \fBrecord\-start\.sh\fR are invoked simultaneously and the PID file does not yet exist, both may pass the running check and launch duplicate instances of \fBRECORD_START_PROGRAM\fR, with the second \fBecho $! > $RECORD_PID_FILE\fR silently overwriting the first\.
.P
If \fBRECORD_START_PROGRAM\fR is run multiple times on the same date, each run appends to the same datestamped \fB\.out\fR file rather than creating a new one, as \fB>\fR is used but stdout and stderr from successive runs will be interleaved without any separator\.
.P
The PID file is written after \fBnohup\fR returns the background PID, but there is no check that \fBRECORD_START_PROGRAM\fR actually started successfully\. If it exits immediately, the PID file will contain a stale PID and subsequent tools will behave as if the program is still running until the PID is recycled by the OS\.
.P
The confirmation message is written to standard error rather than standard output, which is consistent with the rest of the RECORD tools but means it will be suppressed if stderr is redirected\.
